; 03-implies.kurt

; `implies` is Kurt's built-in logical implication, declared as an infix
; operator right inside the core language (see `minimal.kurt`, hard-coded and
; loaded before anything else).  Given `A implies B` and `A`, Kurt can derive
; `B` -- the rule is called "impl-elim", better known as modus ponens.

bool A, B

; `B` below is derived automatically via "impl-elim" (modus ponens), logged as e.g.
; "by 3, 2" -- the two premises' line numbers, not the rule's name (unlike an axiom
; with its own `use ... "label"`).
use A implies B
use A
B

; Chains of implications work fine too, one step at a time:
bool C
use B implies C
C                       ; uses the just-derived `B` together with `B implies C`

; Proving an implication itself (instead of just using one as an axiom) needs
; `show`, `proof`, `assume` and `qed` -- see lessons 07-09 and 20.
